<worksheet xml:id="hw-06">
    <title>Homework 06</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            For each part of the problem set up the corresponding math concepts, use techniques that we discussed and then double check everything with MATLAB.
        </p>
            <p>
            The MATLAB code at the end of the homework can be used as a template.
    
</p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="civil-engineering-problem">
      

<title>Highway Drainage and Stormwater Management Design Project</title>

<introduction>
<p>
A growing community is constructing a new highway interchange through an area that regularly experiences flooding during heavy rainstorms. Civil engineers must determine whether the proposed roadway and stormwater retention pond will adequately manage runoff while remaining within budget.
</p>

<p>
The roadway elevation is modeled by
</p>

<md>
h(x,y)=100+0.3x-0.1y+0.02xy
</md>

<p>
where <m>h(x,y)</m> is measured in meters.
</p>

<p>
The retention pond depth is modeled by
</p>

<md>
z(x,y)=8-\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{y^2}{b^2}
</md>

<p>
over the elliptical region
</p>

<md>
\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\frac{y^2}{b^2}\le 1.
</md>

<p>
The city has imposed the land-use constraint
<m>ab=20</m>.
</p>

<p>
A major storm is expected to generate <m>240\;m^3</m> of runoff.
</p>

<p>
Excavation cost is modeled by
<m>C_E=50V</m>,
where <m>V</m> is the pond volume. Roadway grading cost is modeled by
</p>

<md>
C_G=5000+1200G,
</md>

<p>
where <m>G=\|\nabla h\|</m> evaluated at <m>(0,0)</m>. The total project cost is
</p>

<md>
C_T=C_E+C_G.
</md>

<p>
The project budget is $20,000.
</p>
</introduction>

    </subsection>

<exercise xml:id="civil-engineering-modeling">
<task workspace="true">
<title>Part 1: Roadway Surface Analysis</title>

<p>
Compute the first-order partial derivatives of the roadway surface and determine the gradient vector <m>\nabla h</m>.
</p>

<p>
Evaluate the gradient at <m>(0,0)</m>.
</p>

<p>
Interpret your results by determining:
</p>

<ul>
<li>the direction of steepest ascent,</li>
<li>the direction water will naturally flow,</li>
<li>appropriate locations for drainage structures or culverts.</li>
</ul>
</task>

<workspace/>

<task workspace="true">
<title>Part 2: Retention Pond Analysis</title>

<p>
Compute
<m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial x}</m>
and
<m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}</m>.
</p>

<p>
Locate the critical point(s) of the pond surface and determine the maximum depth.
</p>

<p>
Use your derivatives to discuss:
</p>

<ul>
<li>where excavation is deepest,</li>
<li>where side slopes are steepest,</li>
<li>engineering implications for safety and maintenance.</li>
</ul>
</task>

<workspace/>

<task workspace="true">
<title>Part 3: Stormwater Storage Capacity</title>

<p>
Develop a double-integral model for the pond volume:
</p>

<md>
V=\iint_R z(x,y)\,dA.
</md>

<p>
Use the transformation
</p>

<md>
x=ar\cos\theta,\qquad y=br\sin\theta
</md>

<p>
to simplify the integral.
</p>

<p>
to rewrite the integral in terms of <m> r</m> and <m>>\theta</m>.
For this transformation, the area element (taking into account the Jacobian) is
</p>
<md>
dA=ab\,r\,drd\theta.
</md>
<p>
State whether the proposed pond provides sufficient flood protection.
</p>
</task>

<workspace/>

<task workspace="true">
<title>Part 4: Pond Design Optimization</title>

<p>
The city would like to know whether changing the shape of the pond could increase storage while keeping the same available land area.
</p>

<p>
Express the pond volume in terms of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
</p>

<p>
Apply the constraint
<m>ab=20</m>
and determine whether changing the shape of the ellipse affects storage capacity.
</p>

<p>
Discuss at least two practical engineering factors that may influence the final choice of pond geometry.
</p>
</task>

<workspace/>

<task workspace="true">
<title>Part 5: Cost Analysis</title>

<p>
Evaluate the gradient magnitude
</p>

<md>
G=\|\nabla h(0,0)\|
</md>

<p>
Use this result and your volume calculation to determine:
</p>

<ul>
<li><m>C_G=5000+1200G</m>,</li>
<li><m>C_E=50V</m>,</li>
<li><m>C_T=C_G+C_E</m>.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Compare the total project cost to the budget of $20,000 and determine whether the design is economically feasible.
</p>

<p>
Identify which component contributes most significantly to the overall project cost.
</p>
</task>

<workspace/>

<task workspace="true">
<title>Part 6: Engineering Recommendation</title>

<p>
Prepare a final engineering recommendation for the city.
</p>

<p>
Your recommendation should address:
</p>

<ol>
<li>How roadway topography affects drainage patterns.</li>
<li>Locations for storm drains or culverts.</li>
<li>Whether the pond can manage the designed storm.</li>
<li>Whether the project satisfies budget constraints.</li>
<li>Whether the city should approve, modify, or redesign the proposed system.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Support your recommendation using the mathematical results obtained in the previous parts.
</p>
</task>

<workspace/>

<task workspace="true">
<title>Extension Challenge</title>

<p>
Suppose engineers can increase the maximum pond depth from <m>8</m> meters to <m>d</m> meters.
</p>

<p>
The pond is now modeled by
</p>

<md>
<m>z(x,y)=d-\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{y^2}{b^2}</m>.
</md>

<p>
Develop a formula for the volume <m>V(d)</m>.
</p>

<p>
Determine the minimum value of <m>d</m> needed to store <m>400\;m^3</m> of stormwater while keeping construction costs as low as possible.
</p>

<p>
Provide both a mathematical and engineering justification for your conclusion.
</p>
</task>

<workspace/>

</exercise>
<p>
    <cd>
        %% Civil Engineering Design Project
% Highway Drainage and Stormwater Management
% Multivariable Calculus Modeling Activity

clear; clc; close all

%% GIVEN INFORMATION

% Pond parameters
a = ?;      % semi-major axis (m)
b = ?;      % semi-minor axis (m)

% Runoff volume (m^3)
runoff = ?;

%% PART 1: ROADWAY SURFACE ANALYSIS

syms x y

% Roadway elevation function
h = ?;

% Compute partial derivatives
hx = diff(h,x);
hy = diff(h,y);

disp('dh/dx =')
pretty(hx)

disp('dh/dy =')
pretty(hy)

% Gradient vector
grad_h = [hx hy];

disp('Gradient vector:')
disp(grad_h)

% Evaluate gradient at (0,0)
grad0 = subs(grad_h,[x y],[0 0]);

disp('Gradient at (0,0):')
disp(vpa(grad0))

% Magnitude of gradient
G = norm(double(grad0));

fprintf('Gradient magnitude = %.4f\n',G);

%% PART 2: POND SURFACE ANALYSIS

syms x y a b

z = ?;

zx = diff(z,x);
zy = diff(z,y);

disp('dz/dx =')
pretty(zx)

disp('dz/dy =')
pretty(zy)

% Find critical points
critical_x = solve(zx==0,x);
critical_y = solve(zy==0,y);

disp('Critical point:')
disp([critical_x critical_y])

%% PART 3: POND VOLUME

syms r theta a b

% Adjusted polar coordinate transformation for an ellipse
x_r = ?;
y_r = ?;

% Define transformed depth function
z_r = ?;

% Jacobian which is a part of the dA
J = a*b*r;

disp('Jacobian =')
pretty(J)

% Volume integral
V = int(int(z_r*J,r,0,1),theta,0,2*pi);

disp('Volume formula:')
disp(simplify(V))

%% Evaluate volume for a = 5 and b = 4

V_num = double(subs(V,[a b],[5 4]));

fprintf('Pond volume = %.2f m^3\n',V_num);

%% Compare with runoff

if V_num > runoff
    disp('Pond capacity exceeds runoff volume.')
else
    disp('Additional storage is required.')
end

%% PART 4: DESIGN OPTIMIZATION

syms a b

V_ab = simplify(V);

disp('Volume in terms of a and b:')
pretty(V_ab)

% Apply land-use constraint ab = 20
V_constraint = simplify(subs(V_ab,a,20/b));

disp('Volume with constraint ab = 20:')
pretty(V_constraint)

%% PART 5: COST ANALYSIS

% Recompute numerical values
a = ?;
b = ?;

V_num = double(subs(V,[a b],[5 4]));

% Gradient magnitude found in Part 1
G = ?;

% Cost calculations
CG = ?;
CE = ?;
CT = CG + CE;

fprintf('\nRoadway grading cost = $%.2f\n',CG);
fprintf('Excavation cost      = $%.2f\n',CE);
fprintf('Total project cost   = $%.2f\n',CT);

budget = ?;

if le(CT,budget) % CT is less than or equal to budget
    disp('Project is within budget.')
else
    disp('Project exceeds budget.')
end

%% PART 6: VISUALIZATION

% Create a surface plot of the pond

a = ?;
b = ?;

[X,Y] = meshgrid(linspace(-a,a,100), ...
                 linspace(-b,b,100));

Z = ?;

% Restrict to elliptical region
mask = X.^2/a^2 + Y.^2/b^2 &lt;= 1;
Z(~mask) = NaN;

figure
surf(X,Y,Z)
xlabel('x (m)')
ylabel('y (m)')
zlabel('Depth (m)')
title('Retention Pond Surface')
shading interp
colorbar

%% EXTENSION CHALLENGE

syms d a b

% New pond depth model
z_ext = d - r^2;

Vd = int(int(z_ext*(a*b*r),r,0,1),theta,0,2*pi);

Vd = simplify(Vd);

disp('Volume as a function of d:')
pretty(Vd)

% Apply land-use constraint ab = 20
Vd20 = simplify(subs(Vd,a,20/b));

disp('Volume under constraint ab = 20:')
pretty(Vd20)

% Solve for minimum d when V = 400 m^3
d_required = solve(Vd20 == 400,d);

disp('Required pond depth:')
disp(vpa(d_required))
</cd>
</p>
</worksheet>
